# The blog index moved from public/blog/index.html (the old Astro page) to the
# app router, which exports blog.html and answers at /blog with no trailing
# slash. Deleting the old file is what stops the two from both returning 200
# with different titles and different self-referencing canonicals; this line is
# what makes the slashed form's behaviour ours rather than a Pages default we
# would be guessing at. The 53 legacy posts that linked to /blog/ now link to
# /blog directly, so this catches external links and bookmarks only.
/blog/ /blog 301

# The blog used to live at /insights. Those URLs have external links and
# rankings, so they keep resolving.
/insights/* /blog/:splat 301

# /dashboard was the pre-rebuild member screen. It is deleted: it read a single
# localStorage blob, had no entitlement gate, and its programme ran out after
# seven days. The member app is /app. Old bookmarks, the old Stripe billing
# return_url and any ad landing page that still points here go there instead.
/dashboard /app 301
/dashboard/* /app 301

# The terms moved from public/terms-of-use.html to the app router, which exports
# terms.html and answers at /terms. The old file is deleted, so without these
# two lines every existing link to it 404s, and those links are not ours to fix:
# the App Store listing, the old paywall, 53 legacy blog footers and anything a
# member bookmarked. Both spellings are listed because Cloudflare Pages only
# folds ".html" onto the extensionless form when the .html file still exists in
# the output, and it does not any more.
#
# /privacy-policy needs no line here. The app router exports privacy-policy.html
# at the same path the old file occupied, so Pages keeps serving /privacy-policy
# and keeps 308ing /privacy-policy.html onto it, exactly as it did before. The
# URL Apple has on file still resolves; what changed is which product's policy
# is behind it.
/terms-of-use /terms 301
/terms-of-use.html /terms 301
